The change is close, but there is one security gap. In `checkHost` (`internal/webhooks/webhooks.go`, the `netip.ParseAddr` branch), an IP literal with an IPv6 zone is accepted even when it is non-public. `netip.Prefix.Contains` returns false for any address that has a zone, so `publicAddr` returns true for it. For example, `https://[fe80::1%25eth0]/` has host `fe80::1%eth0`, which parses with a zone and passes the link-local check. The same gap applies at delivery, since `send` calls `checkHost` on the same host. I could not read the Go standard library source to confirm `Contains` in this environment, so this rests on my recollection of it. The fix is to strip the zone before the range check, for example `ip = ip.Unmap().WithZone("")` in `publicAddr`, and to add a test case such as `https://[fe80::1%25eth0]/` to `TestCheckURL`.

The rest checks out against the task. Non-https, userinfo, and private, loopback, link-local, CGNAT, multicast, and broadcast addresses are refused. Mixed resolutions are refused. The IPv4-mapped form is unmapped. Delivery re-resolves before sending, and its error contains "not public" without changing the endpoint. The tests cover these cases.

